  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\n\nAn upper level ridge continues to strengthen across the eastern\nU.S. today into Wednesday with southwesterly flow advecting in \nunseasonably warm air. Wednesday is expected to be when the peak\nof the warmth occurs this week with highs reaching the lower \nand mid 70s. \n\nMoisture associated with an area of low pressure currently over\nthe Midwest is advecting east along the northern periphery of \nthe upper ridge resulting in a thick deck of mid and high level \nclouds early this morning. These clouds are expected to stick \naround much of the day, so sky cover was increased from the NBM,\nresulting in partly to mostly cloudy conditions. The tail end \nof a warm front associated with this system will lift north of \nthe region today. This front is expected to lead to isolated to \nscattered showers through the afternoon, mainly north of the \nSouthern Tier. Most of higher res. guidance continues to show \nthis activity while the NBM remains largely dry, so the previous\nforecast, CONSShort and CONSAll were blended in to increase \ncoverage of slight chance PoPs. Model soundings also continue to\nshow a pocket of dry air near the surface, so precipitation is \nexpected to be pretty light.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\n\nThe latter half of the week will become more active as multiple\ndisturbances bring rain to the region. The low pressure system \ncurrently over the Midwest will propagate into the Great Lakes \nregion. As it does, this will push a frontal boundary through \nwith rain arriving early Thursday morning. PWATs look to peak \naround 1.40 to 1.50 inches, but this system has trended more \nprogressive and as a result, QPF amounts have trended down. So \nwhile this will be welcome rain, rainfall totals look to be \nanywhere from about a tenth to locally 0.30-0.40 of an inch in \nspots. Instability will be limited, with some guidance hinting \nat 200-300 J/kg of MUCAPE possible, especially toward the \nPoconos and southern Catskills, but with higher shear present, \nan isolated thunderstorm or two may be possible.\n\nA slot of dry air fills in behind the front. However, an upper \nlevel trough is trailing this system and will support additional\nshowers Friday and Saturday as wraparound moisture nudges in \nfrom the north. While there is some uncertainty on how long the \nshowers will last this weekend, high pressure does look to start\nnosing back in later in the day Sunday. With northwesterly flow\nreturning Saturday into Sunday, high temperatures do trend back\ndown into the 50s and lower 60s, but will still be running \nslightly above average.",
